em).** Wrapping the column in a function means a normal index on `users.email` can't be used. The database has to scan `users` and run `lower()` on every row. Pick one of these fixes:\n   - Create an expression index: `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));`. The query then stays as it is.\n   - Store emails already lowercased, or use a case-insensitive type or collation (`citext` in Postgres). Then compare with `u.email = 'a@example.org'`.\n\n2. **`SELECT *` returns every column from both tables.** That pulls in columns you probably don't need, such as wide text or JSON fields. It also rules out index-only scans and increases the data sent over the network. Both tables have an `id` column and likely other overlapping names, which can cause problems for clients. List only the columns you need.\n\n3. **`orders.user_id` may have no index.** The join to `orders` needs an index on `orders(user_id)`. Without one, each matching user triggers a full scan of `orders`. Foreign keys often don't get an index automatically. Postgres doesn't create one, for example.\n\n4. **No `LIMIT` or ordering.** If a user can have many orders, this returns all of them. Add pagination or a `LIMIT` if the caller doesn't need every row.\n\n5. **Uniqueness.** If `users.email` has a unique index, such as on `lower(email)`, the planner can find the one user directly and then use the `orders` index. This is the ideal plan.\n\nI only had the query text. I couldn't check the schema, existing indexes, or table sizes. Running `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on the real database would confirm whether these issues apply.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}